## 1. Launch — autonomous watchdog (recommended)

This mode fulfills the three rules: work until blocked, skip a blocked batch in favour of the
next workable one, and **wait for quota to renew and resume**.

```powershell
cd {{PROJECT_DIR}}

# Run in the foreground (keep this window open):
powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File scripts\claude-night-runner.ps1
```

Run detached so it survives terminal close (capture the PID to stop it later):

```powershell
$runner = Start-Process powershell `
  -ArgumentList '-ExecutionPolicy','Bypass','-File','scripts\claude-night-runner.ps1' `
  -WorkingDirectory '{{PROJECT_DIR}}' -PassThru
$runner.Id   # note this number to stop the watchdog later
```

What the watchdog executes each cycle (already inside the script — for reference only):

```powershell
claude -c -p "<resume prompt>" `
  --append-system-prompt "<skill directive>" `
  --model {{MODEL}} --effort {{EFFORT}} `
  --output-format stream-json --verbose `
  --fallback-model sonnet --dangerously-skip-permissions
```

- `-c` continues the most recent conversation · `-p` headless · `--model {{MODEL}}` best for coding
- `--effort {{EFFORT}}` deeper reasoning · `--fallback-model sonnet` continues if primary is saturated
- `--append-system-prompt` injects the **skill directive** (`ponytail:ponytail` first, plus
  TDD / systematic-debugging / verification-before-completion and repo runtime skills) so the
  agent actually invokes them, not just mentions them.
- `--output-format stream-json --verbose` dumps the full stream (thinking + tool calls + outputs)
  to a per-run transcript at `logs/runs/run-<timestamp>.jsonl`.
- `--dangerously-skip-permissions` no permission prompts (see §4).
- On exit (done / blocked / quota), the script waits **30 min** and retries → renewed quota resumes work.

Adjustable parameters are at the top of `scripts\claude-night-runner.ps1`: `$WaitSeconds`,
`$Model`, `$Effort`, `$PermissionFlag`.

